    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d669">

                <pb facs="0723" n="615" sameAs="frus:frus1927v02/pg615"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.4112/172a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to President
                    Coolidge</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-06-21T13:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">June 21, 1927—1
                                p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>The Root treaties of arbitration with Great Britain, Japan and France expire next
                    year between February 27 and August 28.<anchor n="5" xml:id="d669-f1"/> In view
                    of our agreement to negotiate with France, it might be a good plan to informally
                    sound out the British Government and Japanese Government as to whether they
                    would be inclined to take up similar negotiations. This would dispel any idea
                    that we were willing to negotiate any special agreement with France. British
                    Ambassador has been very curious to know what was going on and I informed him
                    after publishing our statement.<anchor n="6" xml:id="d669-f2"/> Houghton<anchor
                        n="7" xml:id="d669-f3"/> is sailing on Saturday. Expect to talk with him on
                    Friday. Should like to know if you approve my sounding out Great Britain and
                    Japan. As to latter, would do it informally through their Ambassador here.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Frank B. Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d669-f1" n="5" xml:id="d669-f1-content">
                <p>Treaty of Apr. 4, 1908, with Great Britain, <ref target="frus1908#pg_382"><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1908, p. 382</ref>; treaty
                    of May 5, 1908, with Japan, <ref target="frus1908#pg_503"><hi
                            rendition="#italic">ibid.</hi>, p. 503</ref>; treaty of Feb. 10, 1908,
                    with France, <ref target="frus1908#pg_331"><hi rendition="#italic">ibid.</hi>,
                        p. 331</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d669-f2" n="6" xml:id="d669-f2-content">
                <p>i. e., a press release issued by the Department June 11, based upon the
                    Secretary’s telegram to the Ambassador in France, No. 174, June 11, 11 a.m.,
                        <ref target="#pg614">p. 614</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d669-f3" n="7" xml:id="d669-f3-content">
                <p>Alanson B. Houghton, Ambassador in Great Britain.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
